<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simón Bolívar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1783–1830</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as the Liberator</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simón Bolívar was a Venezuelan leader of the wars of independence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, known as the Liberator. After the fall of the First and Second Venezuelan republics he regrouped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion</a> supplied him in return for a promise to free slaves. His campaigns liberated Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, Ecuador, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. He was President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia from 1819 until shortly before his death in 1830.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar.jpg" alt="Simón Bolívar" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luis Enrique Toro Moreno (1897-1933) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sim%C3%B3n_Bol%C3%ADvar._Toro_Moreno,_Luis._1922,_Legislative_Palace,_La_Paz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
